issues.problematic_code_snippet:
if dialect.name == "postgresql":
    # Use the trigger's UUID as the lock key
    # pg_advisory_xact_lock takes a bigint, so we use the UUID's int representation
    # truncated to fit (collision is extremely unlikely and benign)
    lock_key = hash(str(trigger.id)) % (2**63)
    await session.execute(
        sa.text("SELECT pg_advisory_xact_lock(:key)"), {"key": lock_key}
    )

# Clear by firing id, and only proceed if we won the race to claim them.
# This prevents double-firing when multiple workers evaluate concurrently.
deleted_ids = await clear_child_firings(
    session, trigger, firing_ids=list(firing_ids)
)

if len(deleted_ids) != len(firing_ids):

result = await session.execute(
    sa.delete(db.CompositeTriggerChildFiring)
    .filter(
        db.CompositeTriggerChildFiring.automation_id == trigger.automation.id,
        db.CompositeTriggerChildFiring.parent_trigger_id == trigger.id,
        db.CompositeTriggerChildFiring.child_firing_id.in_(firing_ids),
    )
    .returning(db.CompositeTriggerChildFiring.child_trigger_id)
)

return set(result.scalars().all())

findings.body:
`lock_key = hash(str(trigger.id)) % (2**63)` depends on Python's `hash()` for `str`. CPython salts that hash per process (PYTHONHASHSEED randomization is on by default), so each server worker computes a different key for the same parent trigger. `pg_advisory_xact_lock` then serializes only evaluations in the same process. Two workers handling children of the same compound trigger take different locks, and the never-firing race from the PR description is still possible across workers.

The comment above the line also says the key is the UUID's integer representation, which contradicts what the code does.

::diff{file="src/prefect/server/events/models/composite_trigger_child_firing.py" lines="38-45"}

Use a deterministic value derived from the UUID. A 63-bit slice of `UUID.int` fits a signed bigint and is stable across processes:

::suggestion{file="src/prefect/server/events/models/composite_trigger_child_firing.py" lines="39-42"}
        # Derive a stable signed-bigint key from the trigger's UUID. Do NOT use
        # hash(): it is salted per process, so workers would take different locks.
        lock_key = trigger.id.int & (2**63 - 1)
        await session.execute(
            sa.text("SELECT pg_advisory_xact_lock(:key)"), {"key": lock_key}
        )

A multi-worker test is hard to write in this suite, so consider at least a unit test asserting the key equals `trigger.id.int & (2**63 - 1)`.

The `RETURNING` clause returns `child_trigger_id`. The docstring says it returns the `child_firing_id`s that were deleted, and the caller compares the result to `firing_ids`, which are child_firing_ids.

The count comparison works today only because the upsert keeps one row per (automation, parent, child_trigger). If that invariant ever changes, the count check can pass when the wrong rows were claimed. Returning the wrong column also makes the API misleading for future callers.

::diff{file="src/prefect/server/events/models/composite_trigger_child_firing.py" lines="147-157"}

Return the column the docstring describes and compare sets, which is stronger than comparing counts:

::suggestion{file="src/prefect/server/events/models/composite_trigger_child_firing.py" lines="154-157"}
        .returning(db.CompositeTriggerChildFiring.child_firing_id)
    )

    return set(result.scalars().all())

In `triggers.py`, change the check to `if deleted_ids != firing_ids:`.
